#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:6
#: 10a5431baf6a4b54870aba1375a62047
msgid "Tuning for Autonomous Aerobatics"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:8
#: 22cc88fdde6047158f2ad98ec31d5b8f
msgid "First, be sure your plane is capable of precision aerobatics. It must be able to not only nose up hover in place, but accelerate vertically from a hover, ie have unlimited vertical climb capability. It must also be capable of sustained knife-edge flignt, ie have sufficient side area to fly knife-edge."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:10
#: 21be1026167349bcb4addc1f8e0f2b4a
msgid "Second, the normal ArduPilot Autotune provides a safe, stable PID tune for most vehicles. As such, its not optimized for precision aerobatics which require a tight tune."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:12
#: db4ac1c0b78d4927baaa787ef1fd5dd6
msgid "This article will discuss how to obtain a more optimized tune."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:15
#: 2f271debe6e04f7e9d0f16b94d6e510d
msgid "Setup"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:17
#: 2a73ad0e42374c03bc6d09defe1b95f6
msgid "Be sure the COG is properly setup. Nose heavy vehicles will perform poorly. Tail heavy vehicles will involve significant repairs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:18
#: eb7b196c07f7422e8b9ba727250b9374
msgid "Set :ref:`SCHED_LOOP_RATE<SCHED_LOOP_RATE>` = 200"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:19
#: 3b2f05d228bf410cb198e196f9f0b9b1
msgid "Set :ref:`ONESHOT_MASK<ONESHOT_MASK>` to match the servos controlling the flying surfaces, assuming that they are digital servos capable of 200Hz signaling"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:20
#: f12b78173955484d8482a9ef0d5d2e20
msgid "Set :ref:`INS_GYRO_FILTER<INS_GYRO_FILTER>` = 40Hz to increase phase margin. Be sure the motor/propeller is well balanced and control surface hinges slop-free to minimize vibration since the filtering is being raised."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:21
#: e85e975ee5904d978e580121cf24b13c
msgid "Depending on the amount of fuselage side area, adequate knife-edge performance may require setting the ``AEROM_KE_ANGLE`` plane_aerobatics.lua parameter to 5-15 degrees to get adequate altitude hold on knife-edges, rolling circles, etc."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:24
#: 26f75d6037274830956b95358799699c
msgid "Verifying the Rate Capabilities"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:26
#: e98f23bc0b9a4e0cb73ad6ebe8563cc4
msgid "The :ref:`ACRO_PITCH_RATE<ACRO_PITCH_RATE>`, :ref:`ACRO_ROLL_RATE<ACRO_ROLL_RATE>`, and :ref:`ACRO_YAW_RATE<ACRO_YAW_RATE>` must be set to less than what the airframe is capable of doing. Values of 180, 360, and 90 degrees per second, respectively, are adequate for aerobatics, but they should be verified to be less than the vehicles's maximum capabilities."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:28
#: 717070adf3fa4368930e89482a48cc36
msgid "Do a flight in Manual mode, with full stick movements in all axes in order to determine the maximum rotation rates that can be obtained. Analysis of the flight logs using the IMU[0].GyrX for roll in radians/second, IMU[0].GyrY for pitch, and IMU[0].GyrZ for yaw log messages will show the obtained rates."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:30
#: 2fdaca0597bd441db01b03b1dc50ec34
msgid "Below is a graph in MAVExplorer of those log messages (converted to degrees/s) from a flight log of a vehicle in MANUAL doing full stick moves to establish the maximum rate capabilities:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:35
#: 95524c2021d24b1892d4902f3540bd68
msgid "The vehicle is definitely capable of meeting the 360 deg/s for roll, while 180 deg/s would be acceptable for pitch (it varies as its looping due to varying airspeed, but 180 is the average), 90 deg/s for yaw is acceptable."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:37
#: 522358ccd0574e6c8dcb2181cade238d
msgid "Note that Yaw rate checking should be done with short stick movements, instead of prolonged full stick deflections to prevent excessive speed bleed off while the vehicle is slipping hard into the airstream."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:40
#: d3b5f411e6a34600adc3b67703444170
msgid "AutoTune"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:42
#: c2c9338caf3d4ea49297f8f0094d5602
msgid "Set :ref:`AUTOTUNE_LEVEL<AUTOTUNE_LEVEL>` = 8, and do an :ref:`Autotuning <automatic-tuning-with-autotune>` flight. Be sure to have set :ref:`YAW_RATE_ENABLE<YAW_RATE_ENABLE>` =1, and tune all three axes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:44
#: 66b096eb3b274bada6607a975ce0f93b
msgid "with yaw controller active, turns will require not only aileron but also rudder in Autotune!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:47
#: 3bf862df8d344fd79e0926cc1591e544
msgid "Check PIDS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:49
#: 723988600efd4abbaa02637d048a6637
msgid "From the the Autotuning flight, check the logs for proper FF PID, and to tighten up the P and D terms. For the following we will look at an actual log and check if how the Pitch portion of the Aututune turned out by comparing the \"Target\" (PIDP.Tar log message) rates demanded by the stick movements during the Autotune for pitch versus what the actual pitch rotation rates (PIDP.Act) where measured by the IMU."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:54
#: 6d0917cc3e424fa89873077f43775dc0
msgid "As the tune progressed adjusting FF throughout the tune, which begins by optimizing D and then P, the matching became better and better, as expected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:57
#: 427630fc41e341d3bd051ba1ff3cfe3c
msgid "FF"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:59
#: e0424cca07b64ec681a0b507af224a8c
msgid "This tune settled on a value of 0.285 for :ref:`PTCH_RATE_FF<PTCH_RATE_FF>`. We can check this by graphing the following using that value to verify its accuracy."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:64
#: 5f98ef75780b400d9e80e07d0b29970d
msgid "since the magnitudes of the two plots are very close, we know the FF term is correct, in this case. If there is a disparity, you can adjust the FF value in the first graph until a match is obtained. This is then the correct FF value for that axis."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:67
#: e5a6a28c2557407b9d6fce25a00fe7f3
msgid "P and D Tightening"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:69
#: c585259d178d4f4c876f07bbc484faf4
msgid "Typically, the FF will be very pretty accurate from an Autotune, but in order to be safe for the majority of vehicles, the D and P values are tuned until instability occurs and then reduced by 1/3 for a large safety margin. This is illustrated in the log graphs below during the Autotune showing the D and P values being adjusted until instability occurs (PIDP.Dmod < 1), and then backed down by 1/3. While this is safe, its not optimum for response, so increasing these values back by a factor of 1.5-2X will increase responsiveness. Be prepared, however, to switch back to MANUAL if instability occurs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:74
#: 35aa59064b094626b53c9872f1bb3fd4
msgid "so in this example we would change the :ref:`PTCH_RATE_D<PTCH_RATE_D>` from 0.0016 to 0.0032 and the :ref:`PTCH_RATE_P<PTCH_RATE_P>` from 0.11 to 0.22 and then test fly."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:77
#: dc5382e927714410a0fd3d49ead2cd91
msgid "Confirming Test Flight"
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:79
#: d3c3be98fb0446b19f0d1420d413bc74
msgid "Do the above analysis for all axes."
msgstr ""

#: ../../source/docs/common-aerobatics-tuning.rst:81
#: 2cbd928062f54acd8e2482dd28681529
msgid "After verifying and modifying the PIDS, fly again, being ready to resume MANUAL control if unstable. A rolling circle trick is one of the most difficult to execute with a softly tuned vehicle, so try that trick. If it does not maintain altitude well, the pitch or yaw tuning during that maneuver should be analyzed and tuning tweaks performed, assuming there is not another issue like insufficient power or fuselage side surface area."
msgstr ""

